\begin{center}\textbf{\small ABSTRACT}\end{center}
\vspace{-0.3em}
{\small\noindent
Financial institutions rely on detailed audit logs to detect malicious insiders, yet those same logs constitute a persistent, identifiable record of the everyday behaviour of thousands of innocent employees, which sits uneasily with the data minimisation and storage limitation obligations of the GDPR. This dissertation asks a specific and answerable question: at what level of anonymisation does an audit log become defensible under the GDPR, and what does that privacy cost in detection performance? We design and implement a privacy-preserving audit log analysis system that first establishes a raw-data detection ceiling using an Isolation Forest over behavioural features, and then applies a formally verified $k$-anonymity generalisation module to four quasi-identifiers, namely identity, timestamp, resource and action, at $k \in \{2,5,10,20\}$, re-running detection on each anonymised view. On a purpose-built synthetic UK financial-sector dataset of 500 employees, 25 malicious insiders and $850{,}853$ log events, the raw baseline attains an F1 score of $0.88$ and an AUC of $0.995$. Under anonymisation, ranking quality (AUC) proves remarkably robust, remaining above $0.95$ even at $k=20$, while the thresholded F1 degrades gracefully from $0.88$ to $0.72$. We show that $k=10$ occupies the practical sweet spot, since every record is then indistinguishable from at least nine others at only $5.4\%$ information loss and with no measurable loss of F1 relative to $k=5$, and we map each configuration to the specific GDPR articles it satisfies. The central finding is that strong, provable anonymity and effective insider-threat detection are not mutually exclusive: the tension is real but quantifiable, and at moderate $k$ it is comfortably manageable.}
