\section{Introduction, Rationale and Objectives}
% ===================================================================

\subsection{Problem and motivation}
Insider threats are security incidents caused by employees or contractors who deliberately misuse legitimate system access to steal data, commit fraud, or cause harm. They are among the most damaging and hardest to detect risks facing financial institutions. Unlike an external intrusion, malicious insider activity is generated by a principal who is already authenticated and already authorised, so it frequently looks indistinguishable from ordinary work \cite{homoliak2019,salem2008}. The economic weight of the problem is substantial and growing: industry studies consistently report that insider incidents take longer to contain than external ones and carry some of the highest per-incident remediation costs of any threat category. In the financial sector the consequences are especially acute, spanning regulatory penalties under the GDPR and the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) framework, direct financial loss through fraud or data theft, and reputational damage that can outlast the incident by years. The distinctive difficulty of the insider case is the base-rate problem: in a workforce of thousands the malicious few are vastly outnumbered by benign staff whose legitimate work can look superficially similar, so a detector that is even slightly trigger-happy drowns analysts in false alarms, while one that is too conservative misses the incidents it exists to catch. This asymmetry is why the present work reports ranking quality and full confusion counts rather than a single accuracy figure that the class imbalance would render meaningless.

The primary technical mechanism for detecting such behaviour is the \emph{audit log}: a continuous, automated record of employee actions spanning logon and logoff events, file access and transfer, email metadata, removable-media (USB) usage, and web activity. These logs are indispensable for anomaly detection and for the forensic reconstruction of an incident after the fact. However, the very property that makes them useful, namely completeness, also makes them a privacy liability. An audit log is, by construction, a granular and persistent record of everything every employee does throughout the working day, and the overwhelming majority of the people it records are doing nothing wrong.

This creates a genuine and, at present, unresolved legal and ethical conflict. Under the GDPR, and in particular Article~5(1)(c) on data minimisation, Article~5(1)(e) on storage limitation, and Article~25 on data protection by design and by default, personal data must be adequate, relevant and limited to what is necessary, retained no longer than needed, and protected through appropriate technical measures from the outset \cite{voigt2017}. Storing identifiable behavioural logs on the entire workforce indefinitely, on the off-chance that a handful of individuals are malicious, is difficult to reconcile with these principles. Organisations therefore face a dilemma they cannot presently resolve in a validated and practical way: they must monitor for insider threats, but doing so entails recording the private activity of thousands of innocent staff.

\subsection{The privacy and security conflict in workplace monitoring}
It is tempting to treat this as a purely legal problem to be solved with policy and consent forms, but the tension is fundamentally technical. Effective insider detection wants \emph{more} data: finer timestamps, exact resource names, precise identities, and long retention windows, because subtle malicious patterns emerge only against a rich behavioural background. Privacy law wants \emph{less}: coarser data, shorter retention, and the removal of anything not strictly necessary. The two objectives pull the same design levers in opposite directions, and existing practice resolves the conflict by default in favour of security, retaining everything and relying on access controls rather than data reduction. That leaves the residual privacy risk intact: a breach of the log store, an over-broad internal query, or a change of purpose can expose the complete behavioural history of every employee. A technical middle path, in which the data is reduced to the point where re-identification is provably hard yet detection still works, has not been systematically characterised for this domain. Providing that characterisation is the purpose of this dissertation.

\subsection{Research question and aim}
The work is framed by one concrete research question:

\begin{quote}\itshape
At what level of anonymisation does privacy become satisfactory under the GDPR, and what is the corresponding cost to insider-threat detection performance?
\end{quote}

\noindent\textbf{Aim.} To design, implement and evaluate a privacy-preserving audit log anonymisation system that satisfies GDPR data minimisation principles while retaining sufficient utility for insider-threat detection in financial-sector environments.

\subsection{Objectives and measures of success}
The aim is decomposed into five SMART objectives, each with an explicit success measure against which the project can be judged:

\begin{description}[leftmargin=1.6em,style=nextline]
\item[O1, Literature review.] Systematically review insider-threat detection, $k$-anonymity and related privacy-enhancing techniques, and GDPR employee-monitoring requirements. \textit{Measure:} at least 15 peer-reviewed sources synthesised into a justified research gap.
\item[O2, Baseline detection pipeline.] Acquire or construct a suitable dataset and implement anomaly detection on raw logs to establish a performance ceiling. \textit{Measure:} a functioning baseline with F1 at least $0.75$ and documented precision, recall and AUC-ROC.
\item[O3, Anonymisation system.] Implement a $k$-anonymity generalisation module over identity, timestamp, resource and action at $k\in\{2,5,10,20\}$. \textit{Measure:} $k$-anonymity programmatically verified at all four configurations.
\item[O4, Privacy and utility tradeoff.] Re-run detection on each anonymised view and measure both detection performance and information loss. \textit{Measure:} a quantitative tradeoff curve with a recommended deployment configuration.
\item[O5, GDPR compliance assessment.] Map system properties at each $k$ to specific GDPR articles. \textit{Measure:} a structured compliance analysis showing the GDPR posture at each $k$.
\end{description}

\noindent All five objectives were met; Sections~\ref{sec:results} and~\ref{sec:eval} report the outcomes against these measures.

\subsection{Scope and threat model}
The project scope is deliberately bounded so that its claims are defensible. The adversary of interest is the \emph{malicious insider}: an authorised employee who exfiltrates sensitive data through file copies, email, removable media or web uploads. Negligent and compromised insiders are acknowledged but not the primary target, since their signatures differ. On the privacy side, the adversary is a party with access to the anonymised log who attempts to \emph{re-identify} an individual by linking quasi-identifiers to external knowledge. The protection goal is therefore to bound identity disclosure, which is exactly what $k$-anonymity formalises. The system operates on data \emph{at rest} for analysis, rather than in real time, which matches how forensic and periodic insider-risk reviews are conducted in practice. Detection is performed per employee over an analysis window rather than per event, because insider risk is a behavioural pattern over time, not a single action.

\subsection{Contributions}
This dissertation makes four contributions: a \emph{constructive artefact}, an end-to-end reproducible pipeline that anonymises financial audit logs and re-runs detection on the anonymised data, with an interactive tool for exploring the result; an \emph{empirical tradeoff characterisation}, the first quantitative privacy and utility curve for $k$-anonymised insider-threat detection over a financial-sector audit log; a \emph{GDPR mapping} that ties each $k$ configuration to the articles it does and does not satisfy; and a \emph{reproducible dataset generator} that embeds subtle insiders and heterogeneous legitimate-outlier personas so that the tradeoff is non-trivial rather than saturated.

\subsection{Ethical, legal and professional considerations}
The project is privacy-motivated, but a monitoring tool is inherently dual-use, and this shaped its design. No human participants are involved and all experiments use a fully synthetic dataset containing no real personal data, so the direct ethical risk is low and ethics approval was obtained through NESS. Two broader considerations were addressed in the design itself. The first is the dual-use surveillance risk that a system anonymising monitoring data could legitimise more invasive surveillance under a veneer of compliance; this is mitigated through the explicit GDPR assessment of Section~\ref{sec:gdpr}, which identifies where even anonymised monitoring may exceed lawful processing limits. The second, following Cavoukian's Privacy by Design principles \cite{cavoukian2011}, is that privacy is embedded architecturally, so the anonymisation module is the primary contribution rather than a bolt-on. Professionally, the work follows the BCS Code of Conduct's duty to have regard to the legitimate rights of third parties, here the monitored employees; legally, it is framed by the GDPR and by the FCA's record-keeping expectations, which require robust monitoring but do not license disproportionate processing.

The remainder of this dissertation is organised as follows. Section~2 reviews the two research streams and positions the work. Section~3 sets out the methodology, system architecture and each pipeline component. Section~4 reports the dataset characteristics, the raw baseline, the verification of $k$-anonymity, and the privacy and utility tradeoff. Section~5 evaluates the results, gives a GDPR compliance assessment and deployment guidance, and reflects critically on the artefact and its limitations. Section~6 concludes.

