\section{Literature Review}
\label{sec:litreview}
% ===================================================================
The project sits at the intersection of two mature but largely separate research streams, insider-threat detection and privacy-preserving data publishing, together with the regulatory literature that motivates their union. This section reviews each in turn and then positions the present work against them.

\subsection{Insider-threat detection}
Homoliak et al.\ \cite{homoliak2019} provide the most comprehensive recent survey, categorising insiders as malicious, negligent or compromised, and organising detection into host-based, network-based and log-based approaches. This project targets the malicious category, which presents the greatest risk in financial environments, and log-based detection, which is the dominant technical approach in enterprise settings. Salem et al.\ \cite{salem2008} and Liu et al.\ \cite{liu2018} survey the detection literature and repeatedly note two structural difficulties. Genuine insider incidents are extremely rare, so labelled training data is scarce, and the base rate of malicious activity is minuscule, so even a low false-positive rate generates unmanageable alert volumes.

These difficulties motivate \emph{unsupervised anomaly detection}, which learns a model of normal behaviour and flags deviations without needing labelled attacks. Chandola et al.\ \cite{chandola2009} give the canonical taxonomy of anomaly-detection techniques and their assumptions. Tuor et al.\ \cite{tuor2017} demonstrate unsupervised deep-learning detection of insiders in structured log streams, while Legg et al.\ \cite{legg2017} build user- and role-based behavioural profiles and score deviations from them. Yuan and Wu \cite{yuan2021} review the deep-learning branch of this literature specifically and observe that, despite strong reported results, almost all of it is evaluated on raw, fully identified log data, which is precisely the assumption this dissertation relaxes. The Isolation Forest of Liu, Ting and Zhou \cite{isoforest2008} is particularly well suited to this problem: it isolates anomalies directly rather than modelling normality, has near-linear time complexity, requires no distance metric, and handles the high-dimensional, mixed-scale behavioural feature vectors typical of audit data. These properties are the reason it is adopted as the detector in this work. Sommer and Paxson \cite{sommer2010} provide an essential cautionary counterpoint, arguing that machine learning for security anomaly detection is often undermined by unrealistic evaluation and by the operational cost of false positives. Their critique directly informs two decisions here: to report AUC-ROC and a full confusion matrix rather than accuracy alone, and to engineer legitimate outlier personas into the evaluation data so that the detection task is not artificially easy.

\subsection{Privacy-preserving data publishing}
The second stream provides the anonymisation machinery. Samarati \cite{samarati2001} and Sweeney \cite{sweeney2002} introduced \emph{$k$-anonymity}, which requires every record to be indistinguishable, over a set of \emph{quasi-identifiers} (QIDs), from at least $k-1$ others, so that the probability of re-identification through those attributes is bounded by $1/k$. In practice $k$-anonymity is achieved by \emph{generalisation}, which replaces specific values with broader categories, and by \emph{suppression}, which removes residual records that cannot be grouped, navigating a lattice of possible generalisations. LeFevre et al.'s Incognito \cite{lefevre2005} is a foundational full-domain algorithm for this search, and Xu et al.\ \cite{xu2006} introduce utility-based local recoding that explicitly minimises information loss rather than treating all generalisations as equal.

Two well-known limitations of $k$-anonymity are important for an honest assessment. Machanavajjhala et al.\ \cite{ldiversity2007} showed that $k$-anonymity alone remains vulnerable to \emph{attribute disclosure} when an equivalence class is homogeneous in a sensitive attribute, and proposed \emph{$\ell$-diversity} as a remedy. Li et al.\ \cite{tcloseness2007} refined this further with \emph{$t$-closeness}, which constrains the distribution of the sensitive attribute within each class. Fung et al.\ \cite{fung2010} survey the broader field and compare these models and their tradeoffs. The present work implements and verifies $k$-anonymity as its privacy mechanism, and treats $\ell$-diversity and $t$-closeness as the natural next layer, discussed in the evaluation. It is worth noting where log-based detection sits among the broader family of insider-detection paradigms. Psychological and sentiment-based approaches infer intent from communications, and graph-based approaches model relationships between users and resources; both raise arguably sharper privacy concerns, since they analyse the content and social structure of employee activity rather than its aggregate shape. Log-based behavioural detection is attractive precisely because it can operate on metadata and coarse aggregates, which makes it the paradigm most amenable to the anonymisation this project studies.

\subsection{Privacy and information-loss metrics}
Quantifying the cost of anonymisation requires a principled information-loss metric. Wagner and Eckhoff \cite{wagner2018} provide the definitive systematic survey of technical privacy metrics, spanning adversarial, information-theoretic and data-similarity families. From it we adopt the \emph{Normalised Certainty Penalty} (NCP) originally due to Xu et al.\ \cite{xu2006}, which measures, for each generalised attribute, the fraction of the value domain that a generalised value now covers, averaged across the QIDs. NCP yields a single, comparable value in the interval $[0,1]$, where $0$ denotes no generalisation and $1$ denotes complete loss of an attribute's information. Reporting a formal information-loss number alongside detection metrics is what allows the tradeoff in this project to be stated quantitatively rather than impressionistically.

\subsection{Regulatory framing: the GDPR and employee monitoring}
The regulatory motivation draws on the GDPR itself and on practitioner analyses of its business impact \cite{voigt2017,tankard2016}, together with Cavoukian's Privacy by Design \cite{cavoukian2011}. A crucial legal nuance, made explicit in Recital~26 of the GDPR, is the distinction between \emph{pseudonymisation}, in which data can still be attributed to an individual with additional information and therefore remains personal data and in scope, and true \emph{anonymisation}, in which re-identification is no longer reasonably likely and the data falls outside the Regulation. $k$-anonymity is one of the few techniques with a formal and testable claim to move data along that spectrum, which is precisely what makes it attractive for audit logs. The Article~29 Working Party's Opinion 05/2014 on anonymisation techniques \cite{wp29anon2014} is the authoritative regulatory reading of that threshold, and it is deliberately demanding: it assesses each technique against three residual risks, namely \emph{singling out}, \emph{linkability} and \emph{inference}, and notes that generalisation-based methods such as $k$-anonymity address singling out well but bound linkability and inference only partially. That assessment is adopted here as the standard against which the compliance claims in Section~\ref{sec:gdpr} are deliberately hedged. Employee monitoring is further constrained by the requirement of proportionality: even with a lawful basis, monitoring must be no more intrusive than necessary, which is a principle that a tunable anonymity level operationalises directly. National guidance reinforces this framing. The UK Information Commissioner's Office guidance on monitoring workers \cite{ico2023} stresses that employers must identify a lawful basis, be transparent about monitoring, and ensure that any monitoring is necessary and proportionate to a clearly specified purpose. A technique that lets an organisation dial the intrusiveness of its retained data up or down, and evidence the choice, is directly responsive to that proportionality test, and it turns what is often a binary policy argument into a documented, defensible engineering position.

\subsection{Alternative privacy models}
$k$-anonymity is not the only candidate technology, and it is worth stating why it was chosen over the main alternatives. \emph{Differential privacy} \cite{dwork2014} offers a stronger, composable and attack-agnostic guarantee by adding calibrated noise to query results, and it has become the dominant paradigm for aggregate statistical release. It is, however, a poor fit for the present problem, because insider detection consumes the individual per-employee behavioural record rather than an aggregate query, and injecting noise into each employee's features would corrupt precisely the rare-event signal that detection depends on. $k$-anonymity, by contrast, preserves truthful records while bounding re-identification, which keeps the anomaly structure intact. A second alternative is pure \emph{encryption or access control}, but these protect data in transit and at rest without reducing what is retained, so they do not address the data-minimisation obligation that motivates the project. A third axis is \emph{global versus local recoding}. This work uses global recoding, in which every occurrence of a value is generalised to the same level, because it is simpler to verify and to reason about legally; local recoding \cite{xu2006} can generalise different records to different levels and typically achieves lower information loss, and it is identified as a promising extension in the evaluation. The choice of $k$-anonymity is therefore deliberate and defensible for this specific data and task, rather than a default.

\subsection{Positioning and the research gap}
Table~\ref{tab:related} situates this work against representative prior art. The two streams are each well studied but almost never studied together, and never, to our knowledge, with a validated quantitative framework for anonymising \emph{financial-sector audit logs} while preserving detective utility. Existing anonymisation research targets static microdata such as census or medical tables, not the high-volume, behavioural, per-entity-aggregated data that insider detection consumes; existing insider-detection research evaluates on raw data and does not ask what happens once the data is made private. This dissertation closes that gap by measuring both sides of the tradeoff on the same pipeline and expressing the result in terms a compliance function can act on.

