\section{System Design and Methods}
% ===================================================================
This section describes the methodology, the system architecture, and each component of the pipeline in the order in which data flows through it.

\subsection{Methodology}
The project follows a \emph{constructive development and experimental evaluation} methodology: an artefact is built and then used as the instrument of a controlled experiment. This suits a causal research question asking how a design parameter ($k$) affects two measurable outcomes. The work proceeds in four phases, namely dataset construction, baseline detection, anonymisation and tradeoff evaluation, corresponding to objectives O2 through O4. All code and data are version-controlled and every step is seeded, so the reported numbers are exactly reproducible.

\subsection{System architecture}
The system is organised as a linear pipeline of five components, shown in the box below. A \emph{data layer} stores the unified log and employee table in a relational database; a \emph{feature engineering} component aggregates raw events into a per-employee behavioural vector; an \emph{anonymisation} component applies $k$-anonymity generalisation and suppression and verifies the result; a \emph{detection} component fits the Isolation Forest and produces anomaly scores; and a \emph{presentation} component built in Streamlit exposes both views, the detection results and the tradeoff to an analyst. The anonymisation component sits deliberately between the data layer and detection, so that a deployment can feed detection from anonymised rather than raw data, which is the operational scenario the experiment evaluates.

