{\footnotesize\ttfamily Unified log (DB) $\rightarrow$ Feature engineering (per employee) $\rightarrow$ [Anonymisation: generalise + suppress + verify $k$] $\rightarrow$ Isolation Forest $\rightarrow$ Scores, metrics, tradeoff $\rightarrow$ Streamlit dashboard}
\end{mdframed}

\subsection{Dataset design and rationale}
Real financial audit logs cannot lawfully be released for research, and the widely used CERT insider-threat corpus \cite{glasser2013}, though valuable, is US-oriented and not tailored to a UK financial institution's role structure or GDPR framing. We therefore generated a purpose-built synthetic dataset whose schema mirrors an enterprise unified log. The generator is seeded for reproducibility and is deliberately engineered so that the privacy and utility tradeoff is non-trivial, through two design features that stress the detector.

First, the normal population is heterogeneous. Alongside regular staff, the generator injects legitimate-outlier archetypes: workaholics with high after-hours activity, IT administrators with heavy USB and privileged access, heavy data analysts with high copy and volume rates, and external-facing staff with a high external-web ratio. These create genuine false-positive pressure, so the raw baseline lands realistically below a perfect score rather than trivially separating the classes. Second, insiders are subtle. Their malicious behaviour is small-chunk exfiltration, mostly hidden inside office hours, drawn from rare resource names that fall into small equivalence classes, so that stronger anonymisation suppresses proportionally more of their distinguishing signal. That is exactly the effect the experiment is designed to measure. Table~\ref{tab:dataset} summarises the resulting corpus.

