\subsection{Evaluation metrics}
Detection is a binary classification of each employee as insider or normal, and the metrics are defined from the confusion-matrix counts of true positives (TP), false positives (FP) and false negatives (FN). Precision, given in Equation~\eqref{eq:precision}, is the fraction of flagged employees who are genuine insiders, and recall, given in Equation~\eqref{eq:recall}, is the fraction of genuine insiders that are flagged:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:precision}
\text{Precision}=\frac{\text{TP}}{\text{TP}+\text{FP}},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:recall}
\text{Recall}=\frac{\text{TP}}{\text{TP}+\text{FN}}.
\end{equation}
Their harmonic mean is the F1 score of Equation~\eqref{eq:f1},
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:f1}
\text{F1}=\frac{2\,\text{Precision}\cdot\text{Recall}}{\text{Precision}+\text{Recall}},
\end{equation}
which balances the two in a single threshold-dependent number. Because F1 depends on where the decision threshold is set, we also report the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC-ROC), which integrates the true-positive rate against the false-positive rate over all thresholds and thus measures how well the anomaly score \emph{ranks} insiders above normal staff independently of any operating point. As argued by Sokolova and Lapalme \cite{sokolova2009}, reporting a threshold-free ranking metric alongside a threshold-dependent one is essential under heavy class imbalance, and the divergence between the two under anonymisation turns out to be one of the more informative results of this study.

\subsection{Baseline detection with Isolation Forest}
The detector is an Isolation Forest \cite{isoforest2008} with $200$ trees and a contamination parameter of $0.05$, which matches the known $5\%$ insider prevalence, trained unsupervised on the feature matrix. The forest isolates each entity $x$ by recursive random splitting and scores it by the expected path length $E[h(x)]$ required to do so, normalised by $c(n)$, the average path length of an unsuccessful search in a binary tree of $n$ points, as in Equation~\eqref{eq:iforest}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:iforest}
s(x,n)=2^{-\frac{E[h(x)]}{c(n)}}.
\end{equation}
Entities that are isolated in few splits are anomalous. In the scikit-learn implementation used here the sign is inverted, so a lower score denotes a more anomalous entity, and the $5\%$ most anomalous entities are flagged as suspected insiders. Because detection is unsupervised, the ground-truth insider labels are used only for evaluation and never for training, which reflects the operational reality that real insiders are not labelled in advance. Performance is reported using precision, recall, F1 and AUC-ROC. Following the critique of Sommer and Paxson \cite{sommer2010} and the measurement analysis of Sokolova and Lapalme \cite{sokolova2009}, AUC-ROC is foregrounded, because it measures how well the score ranks insiders above normal staff independently of any threshold, alongside the threshold-dependent F1.

\subsection{The \texorpdfstring{$k$}{k}-anonymity anonymisation module}
The core contribution is a generalisation-and-suppression module that transforms the log to satisfy $k$-anonymity over four QIDs, each equipped with a generalisation hierarchy shown in Table~\ref{tab:hierarchy}. Formally, a released table $T^{*}$ satisfies $k$-anonymity with respect to a QID set $Q$ if every equivalence class $E$ induced by $Q$ contains at least $k$ records, which bounds the probability that an adversary linking on $Q$ identifies a specific individual, as in Equation~\eqref{eq:kanon}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:kanon}
\min_{E\in T^{*}/Q}|E| \;\ge\; k
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\Pr[\text{re-identification}] \;\le\; \frac{1}{k}.
\end{equation} Identity is first pseudonymised with a salted one-way hash, so that direct identifiers are removed while per-entity behavioural aggregation remains possible, and the role attribute is then generalised. Timestamps are generalised from an exact hour up to a complete removal of time, and the exact timestamp, which is a direct re-identification vector, is always dropped, retaining only a coarse date for temporal ordering. Resource and action are generalised through their natural category hierarchies.

\begin{table}[tb]
\centering
\caption{Generalisation hierarchies for the four quasi-identifiers. Higher levels are more general and lose more information.}
\label{tab:hierarchy}
\small
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}p{2.4cm} L@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{QID} & \textbf{Levels (specific $\rightarrow$ general)}\\
\midrule
Identity & department $\rightarrow$ division $\rightarrow$ any role \\
Timestamp & exact hour $\rightarrow$ 6-hour window $\rightarrow$ core / off hours $\rightarrow$ any time \\
Resource & exact resource $\rightarrow$ sensitivity class $\rightarrow$ any resource \\
Action & exact action $\rightarrow$ behavioural category $\rightarrow$ any action \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

For a target $k$, the module performs global recoding, described in Algorithm~\ref{alg:anon}. It enumerates the generalisation lattice ordered by total generalisation level, so that the least-loss configuration is considered first, and for each configuration it computes the size of every QID equivalence class. Residual classes smaller than $k$ are suppressed, provided the total suppression rate stays within a $5\%$ budget, and the first configuration that satisfies $k$ within budget is selected. This yields the minimum-information-loss anonymisation for that $k$. The result is then formally verified: $k$ is confirmed to equal the size of the smallest equivalence class over the QIDs, so the privacy guarantee is proven programmatically rather than merely asserted.

The lattice for four QIDs contains a small, fixed number of configurations, so the search is inexpensive; the dominant cost is the per-configuration equivalence-class computation, a single group-by over the log and therefore near-linear in the number of events. The full four-value sweep over the $850{,}853$-event log completes in well under a minute on a commodity machine, confirming that the approach is practical at the scale of a mid-sized institution's log.

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=boxgrey,linewidth=0.4pt,roundcorner=3pt,innertopmargin=6pt,innerbottommargin=6pt]
{\small\textbf{Algorithm 1.} \textit{$k$-anonymisation by minimum-loss global recoding}}\\[2pt]
{\footnotesize\ttfamily
1. pseudonymise identities (salted hash)\\
2. for each config in lattice (least-general first):\\
\hspace*{1.2em}3. generalise QIDs to that config\\
\hspace*{1.2em}4. compute equivalence-class sizes\\
\hspace*{1.2em}5. suppress classes with size < k\\
\hspace*{1.2em}6. if suppression rate <= 5\%: select and stop\\
7. verify min class size >= k; compute NCP}
\end{mdframed}
\refstepcounter{table}\label{alg:anon}
\vspace{-0.3em}

\subsection{Information loss and privacy verification}
Information loss is quantified with the Normalised Certainty Penalty. For a categorical attribute $A_j$ generalised to a node covering $m_j$ of $M_j$ leaf values, the per-attribute penalty is $(m_j-1)/(M_j-1)$, and the configuration's NCP is the mean over the $d=4$ QIDs \cite{xu2006,wagner2018}, as in Equation~\eqref{eq:ncp}:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ncp}
\text{NCP}=\frac{1}{d}\sum_{j=1}^{d}\frac{m_j-1}{M_j-1}\;\in\;[0,1].
\end{equation} Privacy verification is independent of the anonymisation step: it recomputes the equivalence classes from the output and reports the achieved $k$, the number of classes, and their mean size, so that the guarantee can be audited without trusting the generator. As a worked illustration, consider the timestamp attribute, whose leaf domain is the $24$ hours of the day. Generalising an exact hour to a $6$-hour window covers $m=6$ of $M=24$ values, a per-attribute penalty of $(6-1)/(24-1)\approx0.22$; averaged with three un-generalised attributes at penalty $0$, the configuration NCP is $0.054$, matching the $k=10$ row of Table~\ref{tab:tradeoff}. A compliance reviewer can therefore reproduce the number by hand rather than trusting an opaque score.

\subsection{Anonymised re-detection and experimental protocol}
Crucially, detection is re-run on the anonymised data using only anonymised columns. The behavioural feature vector is rebuilt from generalised actions, sensitivity classes and the retained flags, aggregated per pseudonym. This measures the operationally relevant quantity: how well an analyst could detect insiders if they only ever saw the anonymised log. For each $k \in \{2,5,10,20\}$ the pipeline records the selected generalisation levels, the achieved $k$, the number and mean size of equivalence classes, the suppression rate, the NCP, and the full suite of detection metrics. The raw baseline is treated as the $k=1$ reference point. Because the entire pipeline is deterministic under fixed random seeds, all figures reported below are reproducible.

\subsection{Implementation and reproducibility}
The system is implemented in Python~3. Data is held in a relational store with two tables, an \texttt{employees} table carrying identity, department, role, seniority and the ground-truth insider flag, and a \texttt{unified\_log} table carrying one row per event with its timestamp, activity type, resource, sensitivity, action, after-hours and external flags, and data volume. Feature engineering and anonymisation are built on pandas, the detector is the scikit-learn Isolation Forest, and the interactive dashboard is built with Streamlit. Every stochastic component, namely the dataset generator, the train/flag split and the forest itself, is driven by a fixed random seed, so a reviewer who runs the pipeline obtains exactly the numbers reported here. The $k$-verification routine and the NCP calculation are separated into an independent module so that the privacy guarantee can be re-checked in isolation from the code that produced it: a compliance reviewer can verify the achieved $k$ on the released data without trusting the anonymiser. This separation also makes the artefact straightforward to extend, since a different detector or privacy model can be substituted at a single, well-defined interface.

