\subsection{Dataset characteristics}
The generator produced $850{,}853$ log events across 500 employees over 180 days, of which 25 employees are malicious insiders responsible for $9{,}206$ malicious events, just over one percent of the log. The heterogeneous normal population behaves as intended: several benign archetypes produce genuinely outlying feature values, which is visible in the wide spread of the normal class in the anomaly-score distribution (Figure~\ref{fig:baseline}D) and in the presence of one normal employee among the ten most anomalous entities (Figure~\ref{fig:baseline}G). This confirms that the detection task is not trivially separable.

\subsection{Baseline detection on raw logs (O2)}
On the raw, unmodified log the Isolation Forest cleanly separates insiders from normal staff. It correctly identifies $22$ of the $25$ insiders with only $3$ false positives out of $475$ normal employees, giving precision, recall and F1 all equal to $\mathbf{0.88}$ and an AUC-ROC of $\mathbf{0.995}$ (Table~\ref{tab:baseline}). This comfortably exceeds the F1 target of $0.75$ for O2 and establishes the performance ceiling against which every anonymised configuration is compared.

