\subsection{What drives detection}
Figure~\ref{fig:baseline} also shows which behaviours the detector relies on. The feature-uplift panel (Figure~\ref{fig:baseline}E) confirms that USB usage per day is overwhelmingly the strongest discriminator between insiders and normal staff, followed by copy-action ratio, high-sensitivity access ratio, and after-hours and external activity. Data-volume features contribute least, which is consistent with the deliberately small-chunk design of the synthetic exfiltration. This ordering aligns with the domain intuition for financial-sector exfiltration, in which removable media and repeated copying of sensitive files are the classic signatures, and it gives the detector an interpretable and defensible basis rather than an opaque score.

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{$k$}{k}-anonymity verification (O3)}
The anonymisation module satisfied the target $k$ at all four configurations, verified programmatically as the minimum equivalence-class size over the QIDs. Table~\ref{tab:tradeoff} lists the achieved $k$, which equals the target in every case, the generalisation levels selected by the lattice search, and the resulting class structure. As expected, increasing $k$ collapses the log into fewer, larger equivalence classes, from $55{,}684$ classes of mean size $14.8$ at $k=2$ to $4{,}442$ classes of mean size $185.7$ at $k=20$, where each class is the set of records rendered mutually indistinguishable. This directly satisfies O3.

