First, ranking quality is highly robust to anonymisation. AUC-ROC barely moves from the raw $0.995$ through $k=2$ at $0.993$, $k=5$ at $0.990$ and $k=10$ at $0.989$, and even at $k=20$ it remains a strong $0.954$. In other words, an analyst working only from the anonymised log can still rank insiders above normal staff almost as well as with the raw data. The ordering of suspicion is preserved even when individual records are made indistinguishable in groups of twenty.

Second, thresholded F1 degrades gracefully rather than catastrophically. At the fixed $5\%$ operating point, F1 falls from $0.88$ at the raw baseline to $0.80$ at $k=2$, then to $0.76$ at both $k=5$ and $k=10$, and to $0.72$ at $k=20$. Across the entire privacy range the detector loses at most four insiders, from $22$ caught down to $18$, so the degradation is smooth and predictable rather than a cliff.

Third, information loss is not monotone in $k$, because the module minimises loss at each level independently: at $k=5$ the cheapest satisfying configuration generalises identity to division (NCP $0.060$), at $k=10$ it is cheaper to generalise time to a 6-hour window (NCP $0.054$), and at $k=20$ it generalises resource to sensitivity class (NCP $0.082$). Detection utility, by contrast, falls monotonically, because the privacy guarantee strengthens monotonically regardless of which attribute is generalised to achieve it. The gap between the smooth utility decline and the jagged NCP curve is itself a useful finding: NCP measures syntactic information loss, whereas detection utility depends on which information is lost relative to the discriminative features, so the two are related but not interchangeable.

