\subsection{The interactive analysis tool}
Beyond the batch experiment, the pipeline is exposed through a Streamlit dashboard that constitutes the demonstrable artefact. The tool lets an analyst load the unified log, view the raw and anonymised sample tables side by side, select an anonymity level and see the resulting equivalence-class structure and suppression rate, and run detection on either view, with detected entities listed by anomaly score and the tradeoff plotted live as $k$ is changed. Presenting both views together makes the effect tangible: exact timestamps and identities disappear while the behavioural summary that drives detection survives. The dashboard is intended for demonstration rather than production, and it deliberately surfaces the privacy metrics alongside the detection metrics so that the tradeoff is never hidden from the operator.

\subsection{Meeting the success measures}
Against the objectives of Section~1, every success measure is met. The literature review synthesises twenty-five sources (O1). The baseline reaches an F1 of $0.88$, above the $0.75$ target, with documented precision, recall and AUC (O2). $k$-anonymity is verified programmatically at all four configurations (O3). A complete tradeoff curve with a recommended configuration is produced (O4). The GDPR mapping in the next section completes O5.

