\section{Evaluation and Discussion}
\label{sec:eval}
% ===================================================================

\subsection{Interpreting the tradeoff and a recommended configuration}
The headline conclusion is that strong, provable anonymity and effective insider detection are not mutually exclusive. Across a tenfold increase in anonymity, from $k=2$ to $k=20$, F1 falls by only $0.08$ and AUC by $0.04$. Insider signal in audit data is carried by aggregate behavioural rates such as USB usage, copy ratios and after-hours proportion, and these survive generalisation of the underlying categorical QIDs far better than a re-identification attack does. Anonymisation blurs \emph{who} and \emph{exactly when}, but preserves \emph{how much} and \emph{what kind}, which is what the detector consumes.

Within this space, $k=10$ is the recommended deployment configuration. It offers a materially stronger guarantee than $k=5$, since every record is indistinguishable from at least nine others rather than four, yet incurs no loss of F1 (both $0.76$), a negligible AUC difference ($0.989$ against $0.990$) and in fact marginally lower information loss (NCP $0.054$ against $0.060$), because time-window generalisation happens to be cheaper than division generalisation on this data. On this evidence $k=10$ dominates $k=5$ on almost every axis. We would reserve $k=20$ for particularly sensitive deployments or longer retention, accepting the AUC drop to $0.954$, and treat $k=2$ as insufficient, since a re-identification probability of one half offers little meaningful protection despite its near-zero utility cost.

\subsection{GDPR compliance assessment (O5)}
\label{sec:gdpr}
The value of expressing anonymity as the tunable parameter $k$ is that it converts an abstract legal question into an engineering decision. Table~\ref{tab:gdpr} maps each configuration to the GDPR articles it does and does not satisfy, and the reasoning is as follows.

