The raw log is unambiguously personal data and satisfies none of the minimisation-oriented provisions. Pseudonymisation alone, as at $k=2$, does not exit GDPR scope, because Recital~26 is explicit that pseudonymised data remains personal data. The critical transition occurs at moderate $k$: at $k=5$ to $k=10$, with every record indistinguishable within a class of at least five to ten and with direct identifiers and exact timestamps removed, the data has a credible though never absolute claim to satisfy Articles~5(1)(c) and~5(1)(e), and the design embodies Article~25 and Article~32. Only at $k=20$ does the data approach the Recital~26 threshold at which it might fall outside the Regulation entirely. The honest caveat, consistent with \cite{ldiversity2007,tcloseness2007,wp29anon2014}, is that $k$-anonymity bounds identity disclosure but not attribute disclosure, so a fuller deployment would layer $\ell$-diversity or $t$-closeness, and no technical measure removes the need for a documented lawful basis and a Data Protection Impact Assessment.

\subsection{Practical deployment guidance}
Translating these results into practice suggests a small number of concrete recommendations for a regulated firm. Detection should be driven from the anonymised log rather than the raw log, because the evidence shows this costs little detective power while materially reducing privacy risk. The anonymity level should be treated as a governed parameter, documented in the firm's Data Protection Impact Assessment and set no lower than is operationally justified, with $k=10$ a reasonable default. Retention should be tied to $k$: because a higher $k$ reduces re-identification risk, it can justify a longer analysis window under storage limitation, whereas raw data should be retained only briefly. Finally, because the operating threshold is what most affects F1, the detection cut-off should be recalibrated per configuration rather than fixed, which as noted below recovers much of the apparent F1 loss. The cost-benefit calculus is favourable: the additional false alarms introduced by moderate anonymisation are few, rising from three to six across the whole range, so the analyst review burden barely changes, while the reduction in privacy risk is substantial and, unlike a policy control, provable and auditable.

\subsection{Critical reflection on the artefact}
Judged against its objectives, the artefact is successful: all five objectives were met, the baseline exceeded its target, $k$-anonymity was formally verified, and a complete tradeoff curve with a justified recommendation was produced. The design choice that most improved the work's quality was investing in a hard dataset. An earlier iteration produced insiders so blatant that detection was perfect at every $k$ and the tradeoff curve was flat and uninteresting. Deliberately engineering subtle insiders and legitimate-outlier personas is what made the baseline land at a realistic $0.88$ and the tradeoff meaningful. This is a methodological lesson worth stating: for privacy and utility research, the difficulty of the underlying task is not incidental to the result, it is the result.

The clearest reflective insight is the divergence between AUC and F1 under anonymisation, which Section~6 returns to. Most of the apparent F1 loss is a thresholding artefact, because at higher $k$ the score distribution compresses and a fixed $5\%$ cut-off becomes mis-calibrated, so recovering it would be as simple as re-calibrating the threshold per configuration.

\subsection{Ethical implications revisited}
The results allow the dual-use concern raised in Section~1 to be revisited with evidence rather than in the abstract. Because effective detection persists at moderate $k$, the project removes a common excuse for over-retention: an organisation can no longer argue that privacy protection would cripple its ability to detect insiders, since the data shows otherwise. This cuts against invasive monitoring rather than enabling it. At the same time, the finding could be misused to argue that, since anonymised monitoring still works, monitoring should be extended more broadly. The defence against that reading is built into the GDPR mapping: even a well-anonymised system remains subject to necessity and proportionality, and Section~\ref{sec:gdpr} is explicit that a technical guarantee does not substitute for a lawful basis, a purpose limitation, or a Data Protection Impact Assessment. The artefact makes privacy-respecting monitoring more achievable, but it cannot adjudicate whether a given monitoring programme is justified in the first place; that remains a governance decision for which the tool supplies evidence rather than a verdict.

\subsection{Limitations and threats to validity}
Six limitations bound the strength of the claims, and they are set out here in the order of how far they qualify the central finding.

\noindent\textbf{Construct validity: synthetic data.} The evaluation uses synthetic data. This is a deliberate and defensible choice, since real financial logs cannot be lawfully released and synthesis lets us embed known ground truth and controlled difficulty, but the specific numbers depend on the generator's assumptions, and behaviour on a real institution's log may differ. The generator is calibrated against the qualitative structure reported in the insider-threat literature \cite{homoliak2019,glasser2013}, which mitigates but does not eliminate this risk. The direction of the residual bias is worth naming: because the generator draws behaviour from parametric distributions, the normal population is almost certainly more homogeneous than a real workforce, in which team-level, seasonal and project-driven variation would widen the normal class and depress precision at every $k$. The absolute figures reported here should therefore be read as an upper envelope, whereas the \emph{shape} of the tradeoff, which is the actual claim, depends only on the far weaker assumption that insider signal is carried by aggregate rates.

\noindent\textbf{Internal validity: design of the anonymisation.} The module performs global recoding over four QIDs with a $5\%$ suppression budget, and a different QID set, hierarchy design, or budget would shift the curve, while local recoding \cite{xu2006} could reduce information loss further. The hierarchies themselves are hand-designed rather than learned, so the lattice search finds the cheapest configuration \emph{within a design choice} rather than the globally optimal generalisation. The $5\%$ suppression budget is likewise a convention rather than a derived value; a tighter budget would force earlier generalisation and a looser one would discard more records, and neither boundary was explored.

\noindent\textbf{External validity: one detector, one dataset.} A single detector and a single dataset are used, so confirming that the AUC-robustness finding generalises to other detectors, such as autoencoders or one-class SVMs, and to the CERT corpus \cite{glasser2013}, is the most valuable next step. The Isolation Forest is also, by construction, the detector family most favourable to the finding, since it consumes aggregate features exclusively; a sequence model that reads event order and exact inter-arrival times would have far more to lose from timestamp generalisation, so the result should not be assumed to transfer to that class without testing.

\noindent\textbf{Statistical limitation: single seeded run.} Every configuration is reported from one seeded run rather than from repeated trials with confidence intervals. With only 25 insiders, a single additional missed insider moves recall by four percentage points, so differences of that order between adjacent $k$ values, notably the identical F1 at $k=5$ and $k=10$, are within plausible run-to-run noise and should not be over-interpreted. The monotone trend across the full range is robust; the fine ranking of neighbouring configurations is not, and repeated seeded runs with reported variance would be a cheap and worthwhile addition.

\noindent\textbf{Scope limitation: threat model and privacy model.} The privacy adversary is assumed to attempt re-identification by linking quasi-identifiers to external knowledge. $k$-anonymity bounds precisely that risk and no other, so attribute disclosure within a homogeneous equivalence class, and the inference and linkability risks emphasised by the Article~29 Working Party \cite{wp29anon2014}, remain outside the guarantee. On the detection side, negligent and compromised insiders are excluded, and the batch, at-rest design means the results say nothing about real-time streaming detection, where an anonymisation step would sit on the latency path.

\noindent\textbf{Evaluation limitation: fixed operating point.} All configurations are compared at a fixed $5\%$ contamination threshold, which the analysis itself identifies as the main cause of the apparent F1 decline. Reporting F1 at a per-configuration recalibrated threshold would give a fairer picture of the achievable operating point, and its omission means the F1 figures reported here are a conservative lower bound on what a tuned deployment would obtain. We also inherit the general caution of Sommer and Paxson \cite{sommer2010} that anomaly-detection results must be read with the operational cost of false positives in mind.

\subsection{Generalisability and transferability}
Although the experiment uses one synthetic dataset and one detector, the mechanism behind the central finding is general, and any detector that, like the Isolation Forest, consumes per-entity aggregate features rather than exact event attributes should inherit the same robustness of ranking under anonymisation. The magnitude of the tradeoff will vary with how much insider signal is concentrated in rare quasi-identifier combinations, which is exactly the property the generator was tuned to include; an institution whose insiders rely on removable media, an aggregate channel, might see smaller utility loss, while one whose insiders depend on rare specific resources might see more. The framework itself, namely anonymise to a verified $k$, re-detect on the anonymised view, and report both privacy and utility, is dataset- and detector-agnostic.

\subsection{Reflection on the choice of privacy technology}
The results also invite reflection on whether $k$-anonymity was the right mechanism. Its principal weakness, attribute disclosure, is real: if every member of an equivalence class shares the same sensitive value, an adversary learns that value without re-identifying anyone, which is why the evaluation recommends layering $\ell$-diversity or $t$-closeness in any real deployment. Against this, $k$-anonymity has two advantages the results bear out. It preserves truthful, per-record data, which is why the anomaly structure and therefore the AUC survive so well, and it produces a single legible parameter that maps cleanly onto a compliance narrative, which a noise-based mechanism does not. The finding that ranking quality is preserved is in effect a finding about $k$-anonymity specifically: it is the truthfulness of the generalised records, not merely their coarseness, that keeps detection viable.

\subsection{Contribution relative to prior work}
Prior anonymisation research \cite{sweeney2002,ldiversity2007,fung2010} evaluates privacy against re-identification on static microdata, and prior insider-detection research \cite{homoliak2019,yuan2021,tuor2017} evaluates detection on raw data. This dissertation closes the loop between them, and the transferable finding is that ranking-based insider detection is substantially more robust to $k$-anonymisation than re-identification risk is, which reframes the tradeoff in this domain from a zero-sum conflict into a tractable tuning problem.

