\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
% ===================================================================
This dissertation set out to determine at what level of anonymisation an audit log becomes defensible under the GDPR, and what that costs in insider-threat detection. Building an end-to-end pipeline over a purpose-built financial-sector dataset, we established a strong raw baseline with an F1 of $0.88$ and an AUC of $0.995$, implemented and formally verified a $k$-anonymity generalisation module at $k\in\{2,5,10,20\}$, and measured the full privacy and utility tradeoff. The evidence supports a clear and somewhat optimistic answer: effective detection and provable anonymity coexist. Detection ranking, measured by AUC, remains above $0.95$ even at $k=20$, thresholded F1 degrades only gracefully, and $k=10$ emerges as a sweet spot that delivers meaningful GDPR-aligned anonymity, covering data minimisation, storage limitation and privacy by design, at negligible utility cost. The practical implication for financial institutions is concrete: they need not choose between monitoring for insider threats and respecting the privacy of their workforce, since they can select an anonymity level as an explicit and defensible parameter and retain most of their detective power.

Returning to the research question, the study gives it a quantified answer. Anonymisation becomes GDPR-defensible in a meaningful sense at $k$ in the range of five to ten, where direct identifiers and exact timestamps are removed and every record hides in a class of at least five to ten, and the cost of reaching $k=10$ is a fall in F1 from $0.88$ to $0.76$ with AUC essentially unchanged at $0.989$. Expressed the other way round, the price of provable, moderate anonymity is roughly four percentage points of ranking-agnostic detection and, in a ranking-and-review workflow, close to nothing operationally. That is a price most institutions should be willing to pay, and being able to state it as a number is itself part of the contribution.

Four contributions were claimed in Section~1 and each is now discharged. The \emph{constructive artefact} is an end-to-end pipeline that anonymises a financial audit log to a verified $k$ and re-detects on the anonymised view, exposed through an interactive tool. The \emph{empirical tradeoff characterisation} is Table~\ref{tab:tradeoff} and Figure~\ref{fig:tradeoff}, which quantify for the first time what $k$-anonymisation costs insider detection on this class of data. The \emph{GDPR mapping} of Table~\ref{tab:gdpr} converts an abstract legal question into the setting of a single engineering parameter that a compliance function can review and a Data Protection Impact Assessment can record. The \emph{reproducible dataset generator} embeds subtle insiders alongside legitimate-outlier personas, and the methodological lesson learned in building it, that the difficulty of the underlying task is not incidental to a privacy and utility result but constitutive of it, is transferable to any study of this shape.

Two findings deserve emphasis because they are more general than the specific numbers. The first is the divergence between AUC and F1 under anonymisation. Reporting only the thresholded metric would have supported the pessimistic conclusion that anonymisation costs sixteen points of performance, whereas the near-flat AUC shows the detector's ranking is substantially intact and that most of the apparent loss is a thresholding artefact. Since insider triage is in practice a rank-and-review workflow rather than an automated block, this distinction is the difference between a result that discourages privacy engineering and one that enables it, and it is a caution that applies to any evaluation of a privacy mechanism against a rare-event detector. The second is the mechanism behind that robustness. Aggregate behavioural rates are statistics over many events and are perturbed only weakly by generalising the categorical attributes of individual events, whereas linkability, which is what a re-identification attack consumes, is destroyed by exactly the same operation. Privacy and utility here draw on different properties of the data, which is why they are not in the zero-sum relationship the framing usually assumes.

The claim is nonetheless a bounded one. The evidence comes from one synthetic dataset, one detector family and one privacy model, evaluated at a fixed operating point and from a single seeded run, and Section~5.8 sets out where each of those choices could move the result. What the study establishes firmly is the existence and the shape of a workable middle path; establishing its exact position for a given institution remains an empirical exercise on that institution's own data.

Future work follows directly from those limitations. The priority is validation on the CERT corpus \cite{glasser2013} and against additional detectors, in particular sequence models that consume event order and therefore have most to lose from timestamp generalisation, since this is the sharpest available test of the AUC-robustness finding. Beyond that, four extensions are promising: layering $\ell$-diversity or $t$-closeness to bound attribute disclosure as well as identity disclosure; replacing global recoding with utility-based local recoding \cite{xu2006} to reduce information loss at the same $k$; adding per-configuration threshold recalibration, with repeated seeded runs and confidence intervals, to recover the F1 lost to distribution compression and to quantify run-to-run variance; and a direct empirical comparison against a differentially private treatment of the same features \cite{dwork2014}, which the analysis in Section~5.10 predicts would damage the rare-event signal considerably more. Each is a modest and well-scoped step built on the pipeline established here. Taken together they would move the central claim from a demonstrated possibility to a calibrated engineering guideline, which is what a regulated firm would need before setting $k$ in production.

